  "item": "impl[CachedParse/SourceProvider]/fn[read]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This required SourceProvider callback is an error guard: the fixture\n supplies cached snapshots by overriding parse. Structural search must\n use those snapshots without refetching. Calling read would fail the\n contract tested below; primitive reads are exercised by other providers."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/sources.rs#impl[CachedParse/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` is a valid use of the built-in. It is on an explicit function with a body. The commentary is fenced and gives a substantive reason, not just difficulty. The code matches that reason. `read` is a required trait callback that only returns `SourceError::Invalid(\"cached parser must not refetch\")`. The sibling `parse` override in the same impl supplies a fixed snapshot through `parse_snapshot` and never calls `read`. The caller, `structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope`, asserts that all three searches parse through the override (`parses == 3`) and that matches come from the cached snapshot. Calling `read` would therefore break the tested contract. Other providers in the same file (`Memory`, `Primitives`, `FailingProvider`) implement `read` and cover primitive reads, as the commentary says. The citation is a proposal that still needs an accepted independent assessment, and this review finds no gap in it.",
